ng `paths.ValidateCheckpointID()` helper\n- Good test coverage for success and not-found cases\n- Best-effort error handling in `saveSummaryToCheckpoint` is appropriate for the use case\n\n**Critical Issues:**\n1. **Metadata field loss on update** - Line 710-713 in committed.go: Only summary fields are updated; all other metadata fields are NOT preserved when checkpoint ID < 12 characters\n2. **Insufficient null/empty handling** - No validation that at least one summary field is provided\n3. **Race condition risk** - Tree flattening/rebuilding between read and write creates potential conflict window\n4. **Incomplete error wrapping** - Some git operations lack context\n\n**Important Issues:**\n1. Test gap: No coverage for multi-session checkpoints or archived sessions\n2. `.gitignore` change is questionable - see below\n\n---\n\n## Critical Issues (Must Fix)\n\n### [committed.go:710-713] Metadata field loss bug\n\nThe implementation overwrites ALL non-summary fields with their existing values, which works but is fragile:\n\n```go\n// Read existing metadata\nexistingMetadata, err := s.readMetadataFromBlob(entry.Hash)\nif err != nil {\n    return fmt.Errorf(\"failed to read existing metadata: %w\", err)\n}\n\n// Update summary fields ONLY\nexistingMetadata.Intent = opts.Intent\nexistingMetadata.Outcome = opts.Outcome\nexistingMetadata.Learnings = opts.Learnings\nexistingMetadata.FrictionPoints = opts.FrictionPoints\n```\n\n**The bug:** If a checkpoint has multi-session data (SessionCount > 1, SessionIDs array), those fields are preserved. But this is accidental - the code doesn't explicitly handle it. Future schema changes will break silently.\n\n**Why it matters:** If we add new metadata fields later (e.g., `Author`, `Tags`), `UpdateSummary` will drop them without warning.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Explicitly preserve all non-summary fields:\n\n```go\n// Update only summary fields, preserve all other metadata\nexistingMetadata.Intent = opts.Intent\nexistingMetadata.Outcome = opts.Outcome\nexistingMetadata.Learnings = opts.Learnings\nexistingMetadata.FrictionPoints = opts.FrictionPoints\n// Preserve all other fields (CheckpointID, SessionID, Strategy, CreatedAt, etc.)\n// - schema changes will require explicit field-by-field updates, not silent drops\n```\n\nAdd a comment documenting what's intentionally preserved so future developers understand the safety model.\n\n---\n\n### [explain.go:689] Empty/nil summary field validation missing\n\nThe `saveSummaryToCheckpoint` function calls `UpdateSummary` without validating that at least one summary field is provided:\n\n```go\n// In explain.go:689-700\nstore.UpdateSummary(context.Background(), checkpoint.UpdateSummaryOptions{\n    CheckpointID:   checkpointID,\n    Intent:         summary.Intent,           // Could all be \"\"\n    Outcome:        summary.Outcome,          // Could all be \"\"\n    Learnings:      summary.Learnings,        // Could be nil or empty slice\n    FrictionPoints: summary.FrictionPoints,   // Could be nil or empty slice\n    AuthorName:     \"Entire CLI\",\n    AuthorEmail:    \"cli@entire.io\",\n})\n```\n\n**Risk:** If the summary generation produces empty results, we make a git commit with no actual changes (only summary fields are empty, all other metadata identical).\n\n**Impact:** Bloats git history with no-op commits that have the same tree hash as the parent (different commit objects, but same content). This is wasteful.\n\n**Suggested fix:**\n- In `saveSummaryToCheckpoint`, validate that `summary` has at least one non-empty field before calling `UpdateSummary`\n- OR in `UpdateSummary.WriteCommitted()`, detect that the new metadata is identical to existing metadata and skip the commit (check tree hash equality before `createCommit`)\n\nBetter pattern: Skip the save if summary is empty:\n\n```go\nfunc saveSummaryToCheckpoint(checkpointID string, summary *Summary) {\n    // ... repo setup ...\n\n    // Skip saving if summary is empty\n    if summary.Intent == \"\" && len(summary.Learnings) == 0 &&\n       summary.Outcome == \"\" && len(summary.FrictionPoints) == 0 {\n        return\n    }\n\n    store := checkpoint.NewGitStore(repo)\n    // ... rest of code ...\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### [committed.go:679-750] Race condition in tree read/write\n\nThe implementation reads the sessions branch, modifies the tree, then writes it back:\n\n```go\n// Line 690-693: Read current state\nref, entries, err := s.getSessionsBranchEntries()\nif err != nil {\n    return err\n}\n\n// Lines 704-727: Modify tree\nexistingMetadata, err := s.readMetadataFromBlob(entry.Hash)\n// ... update fields ...\nentries[metadataPath] = object.TreeEntry{ ... }\n\n// Lines 731-750: Write back\nnewTreeHash, err := BuildTreeFromEntries(s.repo, entries)\n// ... create commit ...\n```\n\n**Race condition window:** Between `getSessionsBranchEntries()` (line 690) and `SetReference()` (line 745), another process could:\n1. Read the sessions branch\n2. Update a different checkpoint\n3. Commit and update the reference\n4. Our update overwrites their commit with stale tree data\n\n**Impact:** In concurrent explain commands (e.g., two agents generating summaries simultaneously), one update will be lost.\n\n**Current risk level:** Low (would require intentional parallel `explain --generate` calls), but the code doesn't handle it.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Use git's atomic operations or add conflict detection:\n- Option A (preferred): Use `git_reference.Compare()` or verify parent hash matches before setting reference (optimistic locking)\n- Option B: Document that `UpdateSummary` is not safe for concurrent calls and add a mutex/lock at the explain.go level\n\nFor now, add a comment documenting the assumption that UpdateSummary is not called concurrently on the same checkpoint.\n\n---\n\n## Important Issues (Should Fix)\n\n### [checkpoint_test.go:317-359] No test coverage for multi-session checkpoints\n\nThe `TestUpdateSummary_CheckpointNotFound` test only verifies error handling for non-existent checkpoints. There's no test that:\n1. Updates a summary on a multi-session checkpoint (SessionCount > 1)\n2. Verifies that SessionCount and SessionIDs are preserved\n3. Verifies that archived sessions (1/, 2/, etc.) are not affected\n\n**Why it matters:** `WriteCommitted` has special logic for multi-session checkpoints (lines 208-219 in committed.go), and `UpdateSummary` should preserve that data.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Add test:\n\n```go\nfunc TestUpdateSummary_PreservesMultiSessionData(t *testing.T) {\n    // Create checkpoint with SessionCount=1, SessionIDs=[\"session-1\"]\n    // Create another checkpoint with same ID (multi-session, SessionCount=2)\n    // Call UpdateSummary\n    // Verify SessionCount and SessionIDs are preserved\n    // Verify archived session files (1/, prompt.txt, etc.) are untouched\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### [.gitignore] Questionable gitignore change\n\nThe commit adds:\n```\n+docs/requirements/\n```\n\n**Issue:** This prevents tracking the requirements directory in git, which seems wrong for a project that creates requirements as part of development. These should be version-controlled (they document what was built and why).\n\n**Suggested fix:** Remove this line. If you want to ignore specific generated files, be specific:\n```\ndocs/requirements/*/task-*.md\n.entire/\n```\n\nOr leave requirements/ tracked if it's part of the development workflow. Either way, blocking the entire directory seems too broad.\n\n---\n\n## Suggestions (Consider)\n\n### [committed.go:696, 737] Clarify author attribution for summaries\n\nThe implementation hardcodes `\"Entire CLI\"` as the author for summary updates:\n\n```go\n// In explain.go:699\nstore.UpdateSummary(context.Background(), checkpoint.UpdateSummaryOptions{\n    CheckpointID:   checkpointID,\n    // ...\n    AuthorName:     \"Entire CLI\",\n    AuthorEmail:    \"cli@entire.io\",\n})\n```\n\n**Consideration:** This is fine, but it makes git blame show \"Entire CLI\" updated the summary (not the actual user). Consider:\n- Passing actual user email from git config: `git config user.email`\n- Or making it explicit in commit message: `\"Update summary for checkpoint ... (generated by --generate)\"`\n\n**Suggestion:** Add a trailer to the commit message:\n\n```go\ncommitMsg := fmt.Sprintf(\"Update summary for checkpoint %s\\n\\n\"+\n    \"Summary generated via --generate flag.\\n\"+\n    \"Entire-Checkpoint: %s\",\n    opts.CheckpointID[:12], opts.CheckpointID)\n```\n\nThis makes it clear the summary was AI-generated, not manually written.\n\n---\n\n### [committed.go:736-737] Commit message could be more informative\n\nThe current message:\n```\nUpdate summary for checkpoint abc123def4\n\nEntire-Checkpoint: abc123def456\n```\n\n**Suggestion:** Include a summary of what was updated:\n\n```go\ncommitMsg := fmt.Sprintf(\n    \"Update summary for checkpoint %s\\n\\n\"+\n    \"Intent: %s\\n\"+\n    \"Outcome: %s\\n\"+\n    \"Entire-Checkpoint: %s\",\n    opts.CheckpointID[:12],\n    opts.Intent,\n    opts.Outcome,\n    opts.CheckpointID)\n```\n\nThis helps when reviewing commit history - you can see what summary was generated without reading the metadata blob.\n\n---\n\n### [explain.go:687-701] Better documentation for best-effort semantics\n\nThe code includes `//nolint:errcheck,gosec` with a comment about best-effort, but this is a bit terse:\n\n```go\nstore.UpdateSummary(context.Background(), checkpoint.UpdateSummaryOptions{\n    // ...\n    //nolint:errcheck,gosec // Best-effort save - display works even if save fails\n})\n```\n\n**Suggestion:** Expand the comment to explain why it's safe:\n\n```go\n// Save summary to metadata for future explain calls. Errors are silently\n// ignored because:\n// 1. Summary is already displayed above\n// 2. Failure means next explain call will regenerate (no data loss)\n// 3. More important to show results than to block on persistence\n//nolint:errcheck,gosec\nstore.UpdateSummary(context.Background(), ...)\n```\n