revious Review Status (review-01)\n\nReview-01 covered the `--generate` flag feature itself and approved the implementation. This review covers the storage persistence layer that was added after.\n\n---\n\n## Spec Verification\n\n| Requirement | Status | Evidence |\n|-------------|--------|----------|\n| Add UpdateSummary to Store interface | PASS | `/Users/alex/workspace/cli/cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/checkpoint.go:77-98` |\n| UpdateSummaryOptions struct exists | PASS | Lines 80-98: Intent, Outcome, Learnings, FrictionPoints, AuthorName, AuthorEmail |\n| Implement in GitStore | PASS | `/Users/alex/workspace/cli/cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/committed.go:679-750` |\n| Read existing metadata | PASS | Line 704: `readMetadataFromBlob()` |\n| Update summary fields | PASS | Lines 710-713: Intent, Outcome, Learnings, FrictionPoints |\n| Write metadata back to sessions branch | PASS | Lines 716-748: create blob, commit to entire/sessions |\n| Wire to --generate flag | PASS | `/Users/alex/workspace/cli/cmd/entire/cli/explain.go:176-181` |\n| Save after generation | PASS | Lines 177-181: calls `saveSummaryToCheckpoint()` |\n| Best-effort error handling | PASS | Line 691: `//nolint:errcheck,gosec` with comment |\n\n---\n\n## New Findings\n\n### Critical Issues (Must Fix)\n\n#### [committed.go:710-713] Metadata field overwrite is implicit and fragile\n\n```go\n// Update summary fields\nexistingMetadata.Intent = opts.Intent\nexistingMetadata.Outcome = opts.Outcome\nexistingMetadata.Learnings = opts.Learnings\nexistingMetadata.FrictionPoints = opts.FrictionPoints\n```\n\n**The Issue:** This preserves all non-summary fields (SessionID, Strategy, CreatedAt, SessionCount, SessionIDs, etc.) because they're read from the blob and not explicitly touched. This works, but is **implicit and brittle**.\n\n**Why it's a problem:**\n1. Future schema changes (adding new metadata fields) will silently drop them without warning\n2. Code review is harder - it's not obvious which fields are intentionally preserved vs accidentally preserved\n3. If someone adds a new required field to CommittedMetadata, UpdateSummary will create metadata without it\n\n**Example scenario:** Someone adds `UpdatedAt: time.Time` to CommittedMetadata. UpdateSummary won't set it, creating inconsistent metadata where some checkpoints have UpdatedAt and others don't.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Explicitly preserve fields with a clear comment:\n\n```go\n// Update only the summary fields; preserve all other metadata\nexistingMetadata.Intent = opts.Intent\nexistingMetadata.Outcome = opts.Outcome\nexistingMetadata.Learnings = opts.Learnings\nexistingMetadata.FrictionPoints = opts.FrictionPoints\n// All other fields (CheckpointID, SessionID, Strategy, CreatedAt, SessionCount,\n// SessionIDs, Agent, IsTask, ToolUseID) are preserved from the existing metadata.\n// When adding new metadata fields, update this comment to document the\n// preservation/update strategy.\n```\n\nThis makes it explicit for future maintainers.\n\n---\n\n#### [explain.go:689-700] Saving empty summaries creates no-op commits\n\n```go\nif summary != nil {\n    saveSummaryToCheckpoint(point.CheckpointID, summary)\n}\n```\n\nThe code saves even if `summary` has all empty fields:\n\n```go\ntype Summary struct {\n    Intent         string   // Could be \"\"\n    Outcome        string   // Could be \"\"\n    Learnings      []string // Could be nil or empty slice\n    FrictionPoints []string // Could be nil or empty slice\n}\n```\n\n**The Issue:** If summary generation produces empty results, we still call `UpdateSummary`, which creates a git commit with identical metadata (only summary fields updated, all set to empty/nil). This is a no-op commit.\n\n**Impact:** Bloats git history with commits that don't change the tree (same tree hash as parent, just different commit object). This is wasteful and confusing in logs.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Skip saving if summary is empty:\n\n```go\n// saveSummaryToCheckpoint persists a generated summary to checkpoint metadata.\n// Errors are silently ignored since saving is best-effort (display still works).\nfunc saveSummaryToCheckpoint(checkpointID string, summary *Summary) {\n    // Skip if summary is empty\n    if summary == nil {\n        return\n    }\n    if summary.Intent == \"\" && len(summary.Learnings) == 0 &&\n       summary.Outcome == \"\" && len(summary.FrictionPoints) == 0 {\n        return\n    }\n\n    repo, err := openRepository()\n    if err != nil {\n        return\n    }\n\n    store := checkpoint.NewGitStore(repo)\n    // ... rest of code ...\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n#### [committed.go:679-750] Race condition in concurrent UpdateSummary calls\n\nThe implementation has a classic read-modify-write race condition:\n\n```go\n// Line 690: Read current state\nref, entries, err := s.getSessionsBranchEntries()\n\n// Lines 704-727: Modify entries map\nexistingMetadata, err := s.readMetadataFromBlob(entry.Hash)\n// ... modify existingMetadata ...\nentries[metadataPath] = object.TreeEntry{ ... }\n\n// Lines 731-750: Write back\nnewTreeHash, err := BuildTreeFromEntries(s.repo, entries)\n// ...\n// Line 745: Update reference (not atomic with respect to other writers)\nnewRef := plumbing.NewHashReference(refName, newCommitHash)\nif err := s.repo.Storer.SetReference(newRef); err != nil {\n```\n\n**Race condition window:** Between line 690 (read) and line 745 (write), another goroutine could:\n1. Call UpdateSummary for a different checkpoint\n2. Read the sessions branch (gets parent commit hash from line 690)\n3. Update their metadata\n4. Commit and update ref.Hash() to new_commit_1\n5. Our update uses the OLD ref.Hash() as parent, creating commit new_commit_2 with the same parent\n6. SetReference updates to new_commit_2, losing new_commit_1's changes\n\n**Current risk:** Low (concurrent `explain --generate` calls are rare), but the pattern is unsafe.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Use git's optimistic locking or add a comment documenting the unsafety:\n\n```go\n// UpdateSummary is not safe for concurrent calls on the same repository.\n// If multiple processes call UpdateSummary simultaneously, one update may be\n// lost due to the read-modify-write pattern. In practice, explain --generate\n// is called serially, so this is acceptable. If concurrent calls become\n// common, implement optimistic locking using ref comparison.\nfunc (s *GitStore) UpdateSummary(ctx context.Context, opts UpdateSummaryOptions) error {\n```\n\nOr implement conflict detection:\n\n```go\n// Verify parent ref hasn't changed since we read it\ncurrentRef, _ := s.repo.Reference(refName, true)\nif currentRef.Hash() != ref.Hash() {\n    // Someone else updated the branch, our write would lose their changes\n    return fmt.Errorf(\"sessions branch changed during update; try again\")\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Important Issues (Should Fix)\n\n#### [checkpoint_test.go:221-315] Test doesn't verify all preservation cases\n\n`TestUpdateSummary` verifies that SessionID and Strategy are preserved (lines 309-314), but doesn't test:\n1. Multi-session checkpoints (SessionCount > 1, SessionIDs array)\n2. Archived sessions (1/, 2/ folders) remaining untouched\n3. Other fields like Agent, IsTask, ToolUseID\n4. CheckpointID and CreatedAt unchanged\n\n**Suggested test coverage:**\n\n```go\nfunc TestUpdateSummary_PreservesAllMetadataFields(t *testing.T) {\n    // Create checkpoint with all optional fields:\n    // - Agent: \"Claude Code\"\n    // - IsTask: true\n    // - ToolUseID: \"tool-123\"\n    // - CheckpointID, CreatedAt, SessionID, Strategy\n\n    // Call UpdateSummary with different summary\n\n    // Verify all preserved fields remain unchanged\n    // Verify only summary fields changed\n}\n\nfunc TestUpdateSummary_PreservesMultiSessionData(t *testing.T) {\n    // Create checkpoint with SessionCount > 1, SessionIDs array\n\n    // Call UpdateSummary\n\n    // Verify SessionCount and SessionIDs preserved\n    // Verify archived folders (1/, 2/) untouched\n}\n```\n\n#### [.gitignore] Adding `docs/requirements/` blocks version control\n\nThe commit adds:\n```\n+docs/requirements/\n```\n\n**Issue:** This prevents the requirements directory from being tracked in git. But this directory contains task specifications and reviews (like this one), which should be version-controlled as project history.\n\n**Current state:** The review files ARE being generated in git repos during development (they're visible in `/Users/alex/workspace/cli/docs/requirements/explain-task4/review-01.md`), so they should be tracked.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Either:\n1. Remove this line entirely (requirements should be tracked)\n2. Be more specific about what to ignore:\n   ```\n   docs/requirements/*/task-*.md\n   docs/requirements/*/.entire/\n   ```\n\nIf the intent is to exclude generated requirements, be explicit. If it's to exclude agent workspace state, use a different pattern.\n\n---\n\n### Suggestions (Consider)\n\n#### [explain.go:687-700] Enhance documentation of best-effort semantics\n\nThe `//nolint:errcheck,gosec` comment is terse:\n\n```go\n//nolint:errcheck,gosec // Best-effort save - display works even if save fails\nstore.UpdateSummary(context.Background(), checkpoint.UpdateSummaryOptions{\n```\n\n**Suggestion:** Expand to explain the safety model:\n\n```go\n// Persist summary to metadata for future explain calls. Errors are silently\n// ignored because:\n// 1. Summary is already displayed above (user sees results)\n// 2. Persistence failure means next explain regenerates (no data loss)\n// 3. More important to show results than block on persistence\n// This improves perceived performance and handles transient git errors gracefully.\n//nolint:errcheck,gosec\nstore.UpdateSummary(context.Background(), checkpoint.UpdateSummaryOptions{\n```\n\n---\n\n#### [committed.go:736-737] Commit message could include summary details\n\nThe current message:\n\n```go\ncommitMsg := fmt.Sprintf(\"Update summary for checkpoint %s\\n\\nEntire-Checkpoint: %s\",\n    opts.Checkpoi